  "answer": "We should connect electric appliances in parallel in a household circuit because this ensures that each appliance receives the same potential difference (voltage) from the power supply. This allows each appliance to function independently and efficiently. If one appliance is turned off or malfunctions, the others continue to work normally. In a parallel connection, the total current drawn from the power supply is the sum of the currents through each appliance, which is managed by the circuit design and fuses to prevent overloading.\n\nIf electric appliances are connected in series, the same current flows through all of them, and the total potential difference of the power supply is divided among the appliances. This means that each appliance would receive less voltage than required, which may cause them to not work properly or not at all. Additionally, if one appliance in a series circuit breaks down, the entire circuit is interrupted, and none of the appliances will work. This is why household appliances are not connected in series.",
